// SOUNDNESS QUEUE — `refuses`. LEGAL Rust, refused by logosc.
//
// Reading an element OUT of a local array through a SHARED SLICE reference is
// reported as returning a reference TO the array:
//     error [fn f]: cannot return reference to local variable 'out':
//     dangling reference
// The element type is `&'a i64`, so `s[0]` yields a value whose lifetime is
// `'a` and has nothing to do with the borrow of `out`; the borrow of `out`
// ends with the function. Rust accepts.
//
// ONE-VARIABLE CONTROL, measured 2026-09-12 on the unmodified compiler
// (build 4ce36eea9e50fa26 43) — only the READ spelling differs:
//     return out[0u64];                         -> rc 0  (admitted, correct)
//     let s: &[&'a i64] = &out; return s[0u64]; -> rc 1  (THIS DEFECT)
// and the refusal is independent of the `lifereg.B` index-store arms: it is
// identical with `lifereg_indexstore` and with `liferegbrbhop` armed.
//
// ⚠ IT ALSO MASKS A ROW'S ORACLE. The `refuses`-direction twin of this program
// (`y: &i64` elided) is ILLEGAL Rust and logosc refuses it — but for THIS wrong
// reason, not for E0621. A round that read that rc 1 as "the lifereg.B slice
// door is closed" would be wrong; it was, and this row exists so it is not
// read that way again.
//
// ⚠ LEGALITY RESTS ON A READING: there is no rustc BINARY on this box.
// Opened 2026-09-12.
package sliceref_read_of_local_array_says_dangling;
fn f<'a>(x: &'a i64, y: &'a i64) -> &'a i64 {
  let mut out: [&'a i64; 1] = [x];
  out[0u64] = y;
  let s: &[&'a i64] = &out;
  return s[0u64];            // THE DEFECT: refused, "dangling reference"
}
fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
